tor $v$ shown above. Which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $v$ is an eigenvector corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =2$ of $A$\n\nII. $v$ is a generalized eigenvector of rank $1$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =2$ of $A$\n\nIII. $v$ is a generalized eigenvector of rank $3$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =2$ of $A$" 13194139534715 17592186045417 true] [17592186433715 1072 #uuid "72a34fdf-b3e5-500c-96f3-76e3e66d1191" 13194139534715 17592186045417 true] [17592186433715 1074 "A *generalized eigenvector* ${v}_{n}$ of rank $n$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda $ of a matrix $A$ is a nonzero vector such that\n\n$$\n{v}_{n}\\overset{A-\\lambda I\\,}{\\to }{v}_{n-1}\\overset{A-\\lambda I\\,}{\\to }…\\overset{A-\\lambda I\\,}{\\to }{v}_{1}\\overset{A-\\lambda I\\,}{\\to }0,\n$$\n\nwhere ${v}_{n-1},{v}_{n-2},…,{v}_{1}\\ne 0.$ An *eigenvector* is simply a generalized eigenvector of rank $1.$\n\nFirst, we compute $A-2I:$\n\n$$\nA-2I=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 4 & -2 \\\\ 3 & 1 & 1\\end{aligned}]-2[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 2 & -2 \\\\ 3 & 1 & -1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\n- Next, we find the image of $v$ under the action of $A-2I:$\n\n$$\n(A-2I)v=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 2 & -2 \\\\ 3 & 1 & -1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]={{[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{w}\\ne 0\n$$\n\n- Since we got a nonzero vector, we now compute the image of $w$ under the action of $A-2I:$\n\n$$\n(A-2I)w=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 2 & -2 \\\\ 3 & 1 & -1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]={{[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -8 \\\\ -8\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{x}\\ne 0\n$$\n\n- Again, since we got a nonzero vector in the last step, we now compute the image of $x$ under the action of $A-2I:$\n\n$$\n(A-2I)x=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 2 & -2 \\\\ 3 & 1 & -1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -8 \\\\ -8\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]=0\n$$\n\nSo, we obtain the following chain of maps:\n\n$$\n{{[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{v}\\overset{A-2I\\,}{\\to }{{[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{w}\\overset{A-2I\\,}{\\to }{{[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -8 \\\\ -8\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{x}\\overset{A-2I\\,}{\\to }{{[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{0}\n$$\n\nHere, we see that $A-2I$ maps $v$ to $0$ in $3$ steps.\n\nTherefore:\n\n$\\,$ *$v$ is a generalized eigenvector of rank $3$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =2$ of $A.$*\n\nSo, the correct answer is \"III only.\"" 13194139534715 17592186045417 true] [17592186433716 1068 #edb/ref 17592186433712 13194139534715 17592186045417 true] [17592186433716 1069 "q-109518" 13194139534715 17592186045417 true] [17592186433716 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139534715 17592186045417 true] [17592186433716 1071 "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}3 & -1 \\\\ 4 & -1\\end{aligned}],\\,v=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the matrix $A$ and vector $v$ shown above. Which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $v$ is a generalized eigenvector of rank $2$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =1$ of $A$\n\nII. $v$ is a generalized eigenvector of rank $1$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =1$ of $A$\n\nIII. $v$ is an eigenvector corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =1$ of $A$" 13194139534715 17592186045417 true] [17592186433716 1072 #uuid "93f4dbff-c62f-571f-9c49-e5503854925a" 13194139534715 17592186045417 true] [17592186433716 1074 "A *generalized eigenvector* ${v}_{2}$ of rank $2$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda $ of a matrix $A$ is a nonzero vector such that\n\n$$\n{v}_{2}\\overset{A-\\lambda I\\,}{\\to }{v}_{1}\\overset{A-\\lambda I\\,}{\\to }0,\n$$\n\nwhere ${v}_{1}\\ne 0.$ Recall that an *eigenvector* is simply a generalized eigenvector of rank $1.$\n\nWith this in mind, let's examine our statements.\n\nFirst, we compute $A-I:$\n\n$$\nA-I=[\\begin{aligned}3 & -1 \\\\ 4 & -1\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 \\\\ 4 & -2\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\n- Next, we find the image of $v$ under the action of $A-I:$\n\n$$\n(A-I)v=[\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 \\\\ 4 & -2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]={{[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{w}\\ne 0\n$$\n\n- Since we got a nonzero vector in the last step, we now compute the image of $w$ under the action of $A-I:$\n\n$$\n(A-I)w=[\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 \\\\ 4 & -2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]=0\n$$\n\nSo, we obtain the following chain of maps:\n\n$$\n{{[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{v}\\overset{A-I\\,}{\\to }{{[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{w}\\overset{A-I\\,}{\\to }{{[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{0}\n$$\n\nHere, we see that $A-I$ maps $v$ to $0$ in $2$ steps.\n\nTherefore:\n\n$\\,$ *$v$ is a generalized eigenvector of rank $2$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =1$ of $A.$*\n\nSo, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" 13194139534715 17592186045417 true] [17592186433717 1068 #edb/ref 17592186433711 13194139534715 17592186045417 true] [17592186433717 1069 "q-109476" 13194139534715 17592186045417 true] [17592186433717 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139534715 17592186045417 true] [17592186433717 1071 "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{aligned}],\\,v=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the matrix $A$ and vector $v$ shown above. Which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $v$ is an eigenvector corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =-1$ of $A$\n\nII. $v$ is a generalized eigenvector of rank $2$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =-1$ of $A$\n\nIII. $v$ is a generalized eigenvector of rank $1$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =-1$ of $A$" 13194139534715 17592186045417 true] [17592186433717 1072 #uuid "a3d67809-78b0-59dc-9828-ea082eca69bb" 13194139534715 17592186045417 true] [17592186433717 1074 "A *generalized eigenvector* ${v}_{2}$ of rank $2$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda $ of a matrix $A$ is a nonzero vector such that\n\n$$\n{v}_{2}\\overset{A-\\lambda I\\,}{\\to }{v}_{1}\\overset{A-\\lambda I\\,}{\\to }0,\n$$\n\nwhere ${v}_{1}\\ne 0.$ Recall that an *eigenvector* is simply a generalized eigenvector of rank $1.$\n\nWith this in mind, let's examine our statements.\n\nFirst, we compute $A+I:$\n\n$$\nA+I=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\n- Next, we find the image of $v$ under the action of $A+I:$\n\n$$\n(A+I)v=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]={{[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{w}\\ne 0\n$$\n\n- Since we got a nonzero vector in the last step, we now compute the image of $w$ under the action of $A+I:$\n\n$$\n(A+I)w=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]=0\n$$\n\nSo, we obtain the following chain of maps:\n\n$$\n{{[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{v}\\overset{A+I\\,}{\\to }{{[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{w}\\overset{A+I\\,}{\\to }{{[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{0}\n$$\n\nHere, we see that $A+I$ maps $v$ to $0$ in $2$ steps.\n\nTherefore:\n\n$\\,$ *$v$ is a generalized eigenvector of rank $2$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =-1$ of $A.$*\n\nSo, the correct answer is \"II only.\"" 13194139534715 17592186045417 true] [17592186433718 1068 #edb/ref 17592186433710 13194139534715 17592186045417 true] [17592186433718 1069 "q-109488" 13194139534715 17592186045417 true] [17592186433718 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139534715 17592186045417 true] [17592186433718 1071 "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}3 & -8 \\\\ 2 & -5\\end{aligned}],\\,v=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the matrix $A$ and vector $v$ shown above. Which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $v$ is a generalized eigenvector of rank $2$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =-1$ of $A$\n\nII. $v$ is a generalized eigenvector of rank $1$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =-1$ of $A$\n\nIII. $v$ is an eigenvector corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =-1$ of $A$" 13194139534715 17592186045417 true] [17592186433718 1072 #uuid "ab77c5c3-a1c5-5710-a08d-82e66f9fdecd" 13194139534715 17592186045417 true] [17592186433718 1074 "A *generalized eigenvector* ${v}_{2}$ of rank $2$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda $ of a matrix $A$ is a nonzero vector such that\n\n$$\n{v}_{2}\\overset{A-\\lambda I\\,}{\\to }{v}_{1}\\overset{A-\\lambda I\\,}{\\to }0,\n$$\n\nwhere ${v}_{1}\\ne 0.$ Recall that an *eigenvector* is simply a generalized eigenvector of rank $1.$\n\nWith this in mind, let's examine our statements.\n\nFirst, we compute $A+I:$\n\n$$\nA+I=[\\begin{aligned}3 & -8 \\\\ 2 & -5\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}4 & -8 \\\\ 2 & -4\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\n- Next, we find the image of $v$ under the action of $A+I:$\n\n$$\n(A+I)v=[\\begin{aligned}4 & -8 \\\\ 2 & -4\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]={{[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{w}\\ne 0\n$$\n\n- Since we got a nonzero vector in the last step, we now compute the image of $w$ under the action of $A+I:$\n\n$$\n(A+I)w=[\\begin{aligned}4 & -8 \\\\ 2 & -4\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]=0\n$$\n\nSo, we obtain the following chain of maps:\n\n$$\n{{[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{v}\\overset{A+I\\,}{\\to }{{[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{w}\\overset{A+I\\,}{\\to }{{[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{0}\n$$\n\nHere, we see that $A+I$ maps $v$ to $0$ in $2$ steps.\n\nTherefore:\n\n$\\,$ *$v$ is a generalized eigenvector of rank $2$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =-1$ of $A.$*\n\nSo, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" 13194139534715 17592186045417 true] [17592186433719 1068 #edb/ref 17592186433714 13194139534715 17592186045417 true] [17592186433719 1069 "q-109242" 13194139534715 17592186045417 true] [17592186433719 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139534715 17592186045417 true] [17592186433719 1071 "$$\n\\begin{aligned}u\\overset{A+2I\\,}{\\to }v\\overset{A+2I\\,}{\\to }w\\overset{A+2I\\,}{\\to }0 \\\\ x\\overset{A-2I\\,}{\\to }y\\overset{A-2I\\,}{\\to }0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nGiven the action of the matrices $A+2I$ and $A-2I$ on the nonzero vectors $u,v,w,x$ and $y$ as depicted above, which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $v$ is an eigenvector corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =-2$ of $A$\n\nII. $x$ is a generalized eigenvector of rank $3$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =2$ of $A$\n\nIII. $y$ is a generalized eigenvector of rank $1$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =2$ of $A$" 13194139534715 17592186045417 true] [17592186433719 1072 #uuid "c32d62b6-73f0-5c6b-b445-e1066fb0b96f" 13194139534715 17592186045417 true] [17592186433719 1074 "A *generalized eigenvector* ${v}_{n}$ of rank $n$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda $ of a matrix $A$ is a nonzero vector such that\n\n$$\n{v}_{n}\\overset{A-\\lambda I\\,}{\\to }{v}_{n-1}\\overset{A-\\lambda I\\,}{\\to }…\\overset{A-\\lambda I\\,}{\\to }{v}_{1}\\overset{A-\\lambda I\\,}{\\to }0,\n$$\n\nwhere ${v}_{n-1},{v}_{n-2},…,{v}_{1}\\ne 0.$ An *eigenvector* is simply a generalized eigenvector of rank $1.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. Since \n\n$$\nv\\overset{A+2I\\,}{\\to }w,\n$$\n\nand $w\\ne 0,$  the vector $v$ is  **not** a eigenvector of $A$ corresponding  to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =-2.$\n\n- Statement II is false. Since\n\n$$\nx\\overset{A-2I\\,}{\\to }y\\overset{A-2I\\,}{\\to }0,\n$$\n\n \ni.e. $A-2I$ maps $x$ to $0$ in $2$ steps, \nthe vector $x$ is a generalized eigenvector of rank $2,$ not $3.$\n\n- Statement III is true. Indeed, since\n\n$$\ny\\overset{A-2I\\,}{\\to }0,\n$$\n\ni.e. $A-2I$ maps $y$ to $0$ in $1$ step, \nthe vector $y$ is a generalized eigenvector of rank $1$ (a.k.a. an eigenvector) corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =2$ of $A.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\"" 13194139534715 17592186045417 true]] :edb/tempids {"answer-0898420b-4c54-5e1d-97e3-5cc4bfdd8109" 17592186433685 "answer-16957b53-7f2c-5897-8254-366068b7d73a" 17592186433686 "answer-23feb840-9ea0-5b4a-9981-47d41adc532e" 17592186433687 "answer-31251623-175e-5172-b53d-93dabfd51773" 17592186433688 "answer-4123c97a-32f0-5090-ad8f-278e9a0415cb" 17592186433689 "answer-58792cac-2b0d-543d-8e9e-c8057305744f" 17592186433690 "answer-5b612074-81a0-5d6f-aec5-96eeb7af6bb1" 17592186433691 "answer-5c580e8c-17f9-5329-b05e-162735ed92b2" 17592186433692 "answer-5fc5b2e1-1001-57bc-9ebf-e1899b27c976" 17592186433693 "answer-68d2143e-ca23-5a4c-95d7-62217b46497f" 17592186433694 "answer-698b7655-84cd-57ff-b48c-076442522213" 17592186433695 "answer-6a0f6d67-6d78-567d-a365-5c0702b884e3" 17592186433696 "answer-71851cec-299e-55b4-b56b-b60fb7b9e7ea" 17592186433697 "answer-72e1debb-2d7b-595f-85d2-469a31d07a3a" 17592186433698 "answer-85817bc0-a638-5f2e-8f8a-cdeeff6b3d1e" 17592186433699 "answer-974e648d-43b7-5056-bead-1b5c05bf7686" 17592186433700 "answer-adc06e2a-797f-5b5b-b125-a1b83e296a09" 17592186433701 "answer-b5fda644-47ed-58ee-9a8b-8a80fc0d503b" 17592186433702 "answer-c9aab259-7b9a-54be-b6ec-554fd1c11b3c" 17592186433703 "answer-d1cec6f6-74c4-595e-b9a4-e8d9615d9af5" 17592186433704 "answer-dbcccae4-29dd-5752-98f9-f75c35190636" 17592186433705 "answer-e31b4d52-32a8-5673-be5d-c721da9231aa" 17592186433706 "answer-ebc1826a-9a0c-57f0-84eb-fb71a37f6300" 17592186433707 "answer-ef393691-7df5-